= Introduction

// FIRST MOVE, establish research terriotory, show that the general research area is important/central/interesting/problematic/relevant

Over the decades, studies have shown how Exergames for rehabilitation is more effective than traditional therapy due to higher sense of engagement and motivation (@shah2022, ..add more sources from background). Automation of various work processes is increasingly implemented in rehabilitation setting where many functions now being carried out either partially or fully by automated systems to enable data-driven / *quality evidence-based precision medicine patient personalization while releaving/reducing therapists workload (Howard et al., 2016; Legaspi et al., 2024; Figueiredo et al., 2025).*. AI-systems are increasingly expressing agency, which further *elaborates the need to* embrace more-than-human design and reframe of the conceptual space of how systems are designed to share agency between humans and machines are essential (Giaccardi & Redström, 2020; Giaccardi et al., 2024). In the context of exergame-based rehabilitation, therapy outcomes are shaped collectively by therapists (human actor) and AI systems (the non-human actor) and shaped by the configuration of relationship and agency between these actors. Neither therapists nor AI systems are passive actors in a rehabilitation system but active decision-makers that continuously negotiate roles and responsibility across the rehabilitation (Frauenberger, 2019; Kuijer & Giaccardi, 2018). From this perspective, the human agency is central to the process and continuously negotiated with the system.

// SECOND MOVE, establish the gap clearly, mention how sense of agency is not studied in relation to therapists and AI-supported systems for exergame-based therapy or adresses all four main phases of exergame-based rehabilitation

With the increase in automation of process in rehabilitation, the design of shared agency between human and computational system becomes essential, especially within rehabilitation where agency is critical to support therapists work without undermining or substituting the autonomy and expertise (Vidal et al., 2025; Yu et al., 2026).  Despite this, therapists sense of agency in relation to AI-supported exergame-based rehabilitation systems is generally not discussed. Additionally, while existing systems on exergame-based rehabilitation currently do provide therapists  dashboards, data documentation, adaptive session planning, and task manipulation, they exclude the general therapeutic process (Figueiredo et al., 2025; Gagnon-Roy et al., 2024; Eckert et al., 2023; Ferreria et al., 2022). Moreover, they also do not discuss therapists sense of agency of therapists in relation to the system throughout the therapeutic process. Evidently, the existing systems in research do not address the sense of therapist agency or discuss therapists' agency in relation to the AI-supported system across the therapeutic process.

// THIRD MOVE, state the explorative/speculative nature of our qualitative research and how doing what we will do creates value for more-than-human design + RQ
With the aim of addressing gap by _exploring_ how therapists reason about their agency in relation to an AI-supported computational system for exergame-based rehabilitation and how the agency should be shared between the therapist and the system. To do so, a participatory Research through Design (RtD) approach is applied through two main methods: (1) a contextual inquiry conducted through *a case study (Brain Stimulation)* including interviews and observation, and (2) individual participatory speculative co-design sessions. To properly achieve the objectives of this study, the following research questions were asked: 

*RQ1:* How do therapists reason about their agency in relation to a human-AI system for exergame-based rehabilitation therapy?

*RQ2:* How do therapists' preferences for agency distribution between themselves and a system (AI) vary across the main phases of exergame-based rehabilitation therapy (e.g., therapy planning & goal-setting, etc.)

